---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
---

<Base>
  <Fragment slot="head">
    <link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.googleapis.com" />
    <link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.gstatic.com" crossorigin />
    <link href="https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Source+Serif+4:ital,opsz,wght@0,8..60,300..700;1,8..60,300..700&family=Source+Sans+3:ital,wght@0,400..700;1,400..700&display=swap" rel="stylesheet" />
  </Fragment>

<canvas id="gl" aria-hidden="true"></canvas>
<div class="callouts" id="callouts" aria-hidden="true"></div>

<header class="top">
  <a class="name" href="#top">Lucas Burgett</a>
  <nav aria-label="Sections"><a href="#evals">Evals</a><a href="#writing">Writing</a><a href="#work">Work</a><a href="#about">About</a><button class="lights" id="lights" type="button">Lights off</button></nav>
</header>

<main id="top">
  <section class="beat" data-beat="0">
    <h1>I build evaluations for robot policies.</h1>
    <p class="lead">The metrics and statistics that decide whether a policy is usable in the real world. I study math and computer science at Stanford, and this summer I built the policy evaluation pipeline at Parametric (YC&nbsp;F25).</p>
    <p class="live">The arm here is an animated Franka Panda acting out pick-and-place rollouts. <span id="pointer-copy">Move your cursor over its table to choose where the next block starts.</span> Since you arrived it has run <b class="num" id="h-n">0</b> rollouts and <b class="num" id="h-k">0</b> succeeded<span class="num" id="h-rate"></span><svg class="ci" id="h-ci" viewBox="0 0 64 12" aria-hidden="true"></svg></p>
    <p class="small" id="h-state"></p>
  </section>

  <section class="beat" data-beat="1" id="evals">
    <h2>Evaluation at Parametric</h2>
    <p>A policy that succeeds once might succeed 30% of the time. A real evaluation repeats the task many times, records every outcome, and reports an interval next to the success rate, so the number means what it says. This summer I built Parametric's internal evaluation pipeline for robot manipulation policies, and designed the metrics and statistical analyses used to decide whether a policy was ready for real-world use. I also rebuilt the robot telemetry for real-time remote debugging of policy runs, cutting chart payloads by over 100x.</p>
    <p class="live">The sixteen arms in view act out the same task, each with a true success rate of 74%, which the 95% interval should usually contain. Together they have logged <b class="num" id="g-n">0</b> rollouts, and <b class="num" id="g-k">0</b> succeeded<span class="num" id="g-rate"></span><svg class="ci" id="g-ci" viewBox="0 0 64 12" aria-hidden="true"></svg></p>
    <p class="meta">Software engineering intern, summer 2026, San Francisco.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="beat" data-beat="2" id="writing">
    <figure>
      <svg id="stairchart" viewBox="0 0 440 200" role="img" aria-label="Success rates with 95% intervals for four policies across four t-shirt folding levels"></svg>
      <div class="legend"><span style="--c:var(--ink)"><i></i>Matched</span><span style="--c:var(--tape)"><i></i>Policy A</span><span style="--c:#8f9398"><i></i>Policy B</span><span style="--c:#b9bcbf"><i></i>Policy C</span></div>
      <figcaption>Real counts: four π0.5 fine-tunes folding a t-shirt, 30 to 50 blind rollouts per policy per level, with 95% Wilson intervals.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <h2><a href="https://www.parametric.company/blog/calibration-precedes-evaluation" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Calibration Precedes Evaluation</a></h2>
    <p>Written with John Alvarez for the Parametric blog, July 2026. If a task is too easy every policy passes, and if it is too hard every policy fails. We raise the difficulty one level at a time until the policies separate, then spend the rollout budget there.</p>
    <p class="live" id="s-live">The raised tables in view act out a toy version, with true success rates of 95%, 74%, 47% and 20% from level 1 to level 4.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="beat" data-beat="3">
    <h2><a href="https://github.com/lucasburgett/vla-memory-new" target="_blank" rel="noopener">VLA Memory</a></h2>
    <p>The arm here acts out a simple memory task: a light comes on behind one of three squares and goes off before the arm moves, and the block has to end up on that square. VLA Memory, my CS 224R project, targets tasks like this. I trained a Qwen3-VL-4B planner with persistent keyframe memory using streaming GRPO, driving a frozen π0.5 action expert. It reached 31.4% ± 3.1% success on the RoboMME ButtonUnmaskSwap benchmark, 10.1 points above the MemER-IL baseline, with zero human demonstrations.</p>
    <p class="live">In this animation the right square comes up <b class="num" id="m-k">0</b> times in <b class="num" id="m-n">0</b> tries<span class="num" id="m-rate"></span>, against a set rate of 80%.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="beat" data-beat="4">
    <h2>Scam-victim protection eval</h2>
    <p>When someone who is being scammed asks a chatbot for help, does the model recognize the scam and keep warning them after they push back? I'm building a multi-turn evaluation for it, graded against six bright-line rules taken from FTC guidance, with two judges that must both quote the words that break a rule.</p>
    <p class="meta">In progress. Pilot started October 2026.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="beat" data-beat="5" id="work">
    <h2>Other work</h2>
    <ul class="work">
      <li><span class="yr">2026</span><div><b>MetaChat 2.0 evaluation set</b> <span>At Stanford's Fan Lab I built the LLM evaluation set for MetaChat 2.0, a multi-agent framework for autonomous nanophotonic device design, and contributed to the framework itself.</span></div></li>
      <li><span class="yr">2026</span><div><b>Everen</b> <span>Co-founder. A local-AI app for private clinical transcription and note generation: Tauri desktop app, Rust backend, encrypted local storage. We tested it with 10 therapy offices, and 8 joined the waitlist with stated intent to purchase.</span></div></li>
      <li><span class="yr">2026</span><div><b><a href="https://github.com/lucasburgett/codesentry" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CodeSentry</a></b> <span>Reviews AI-written code. Claude's flags only survive when a Semgrep rule backs them up.</span></div></li>
      <li><span class="yr">2026</span><div><b>CoInvest</b> <span>A multi-agent Claude pipeline that grounds each investment-thesis report in live market and news data, and lists the conditions that would disprove the thesis.</span></div></li>
      <li><span class="yr">2026</span><div><b>Latent rectified-flow CT</b> <span>Low-dose CT denoising in 5 to 10 steps with a per-pixel uncertainty map. CS 231N.</span></div></li>
      <li><span class="yr">2025</span><div><b><a href="https://github.com/lucasburgett/rl-reward-tuning-pipeline" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Reproducible RL pipeline</a></b> <span>PPO with seeded determinism; two runs of one config match bit for bit.</span></div></li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="beat" data-beat="6" id="about">
    <h2>About</h2>
    <p>Math major with a CS minor at Stanford, class of 2028. Xfund Ethics Fellow, Startups to Join research fellow, and a member of the Blythe Fund and the Stanford AI Club. I spent summer 2026 at Parametric; before that I was a research assistant in Stanford's Fan Lab and an engineering intern at Platform Science. Outside work I lift, golf, travel, and visit family in Brazil whenever I can.</p>
    <div class="contact"><span class="addr">lburgett@stanford.edu</span><a href="https://github.com/lucasburgett" target="_blank" rel="noopener">GitHub</a><a href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/lucas-burgett-494a98207" target="_blank" rel="noopener">LinkedIn</a><a href="/Lucas-Burgett-Resume-2026.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Résumé</a></div>
    <p class="live" id="watched">While you read, the animated arms on this page acted out <b class="num">0</b> rollouts.</p>
    <p class="credit">The arms are an animation: each rollout's outcome is a random draw against a success rate set in the page's code, and the motion is scripted to match. The t-shirt folding chart uses real results from the Parametric post. Robot model: Franka Emika Panda from MuJoCo Menagerie (Apache-2.0), simplified for the web.</p>
  </section>
</main>

  <script is:inline type="importmap">
{ "imports": {
  "three": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/three@0.180.0/build/three.module.js",
  "three/addons/": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/three@0.180.0/examples/jsm/"
} }
</script>
  <script is:inline type="module">
import * as THREE from "three";
import { GLTFLoader } from "three/addons/loaders/GLTFLoader.js";
import { RoomEnvironment } from "three/addons/environments/RoomEnvironment.js";
import { toCreasedNormals } from "three/addons/utils/BufferGeometryUtils.js";

const $ = (s) => document.querySelector(s);
const clamp = (v, a, b) => Math.max(a, Math.min(b, v));
const lerp = (a, b, t) => a + (b - a) * t;
const smooth = (a, b, x) => { const t = clamp((x - a) / (b - a), 0, 1); return t * t * (3 - 2 * t); };
const mj = (s) => s * s * s * (10 + s * (-15 + 6 * s)); // minimum-jerk profile
const rand = (a, b) => a + Math.random() * (b - a);
const pc = (v) => Math.round(v * 100);
const wilson = (k, n, z = 1.959964) => {
  if (!n) return [0, 1];
  const p = k / n, d = 1 + (z * z) / n, c = (p + (z * z) / (2 * n)) / d;
  const h = (z * Math.sqrt((p * (1 - p)) / n + (z * z) / (4 * n * n))) / d;
  return [Math.max(0, c - h), Math.min(1, c + h)];
};

// ---------------- theme ----------------
const root = document.documentElement;
const mqDark = matchMedia("(prefers-color-scheme: dark)");
const isDark = () => (root.dataset.theme ? root.dataset.theme === "dark" : mqDark.matches);
const lightsBtn = $("#lights");
let onTheme = () => {};
const syncTheme = () => { lightsBtn.textContent = isDark() ? "Lights on" : "Lights off"; onTheme(); };
lightsBtn.addEventListener("click", () => { root.dataset.theme = isDark() ? "light" : "dark"; });
mqDark.addEventListener("change", syncTheme);
new MutationObserver(syncTheme).observe(root, { attributes: true, attributeFilter: ["data-theme"] });
syncTheme();

// ---------------- real staircase chart ----------------
(function chart() {
  const LV = [
    { s: "One sleeve", n: 50, k: { M: 49, A: 34, B: 31, C: 25 } },
    { s: "Both sleeves", n: 50, k: { M: 46, A: 27, B: 11, C: 11 } },
    { s: "Straightened", n: 50, k: { M: 50, A: 12 } },
    { s: "Crumpled", n: 30, k: { M: 23, A: 2 } },
  ];
  const COL = { M: "var(--ink)", A: "var(--tape)", B: "#8f9398", C: "#b9bcbf" };
  const OFF = { M: -21, A: -7, B: 7, C: 21 };
  const X0 = 36, X1 = 434, YT = 10, YB = 156, cw = (X1 - X0) / 4, Y = (v) => YB - (YB - YT) * v;
  let s = "";
  for (const v of [0, 0.5, 1]) s += `<line x1="${X0}" x2="${X1}" y1="${Y(v)}" y2="${Y(v)}" stroke="var(--rule)"/><text x="${X0 - 7}" y="${Y(v) + 4}" text-anchor="end" fill="var(--ink-3)" font-family="Source Sans 3, sans-serif" font-size="11">${v * 100}%</text>`;
  const pts = { M: [], A: [], B: [], C: [] };
  LV.forEach((lv, i) => {
    const cx = X0 + cw * (i + 0.5);
    s += `<text x="${cx}" y="${YB + 20}" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--ink-2)" font-family="Source Sans 3, sans-serif" font-size="12.5">${lv.s}</text><text x="${cx}" y="${YB + 36}" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--ink-3)" font-family="Source Sans 3, sans-serif" font-size="11">n = ${lv.n}</text>`;
    for (const id in lv.k) {
      const k = lv.k[id], [lo, hi] = wilson(k, lv.n), x = cx + OFF[id];
      s += `<line x1="${x}" x2="${x}" y1="${Y(hi)}" y2="${Y(lo)}" stroke="${COL[id]}" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round" opacity=".35"/>`;
      pts[id].push([x, Y(k / lv.n)]);
    }
  });
  for (const id in pts) {
    s += `<path d="${pts[id].map((p, j) => (j ? "L" : "M") + p[0].toFixed(1) + " " + p[1].toFixed(1)).join("")}" fill="none" stroke="${COL[id]}" stroke-width="1.3" opacity=".7"/>`;
    for (const [x, y] of pts[id]) s += `<circle cx="${x}" cy="${y}" r="3.6" fill="${COL[id]}" stroke="var(--bg)" stroke-width="1.5"/>`;
  }
  $("#stairchart").innerHTML = s;
})();

const coarse = matchMedia("(pointer: coarse)").matches;
if (coarse) $("#pointer-copy").textContent = "Tap its table to choose where the next block starts.";

// ---------------- WebGL ----------------
const canvas = $("#gl");
let renderer;
try { renderer = new THREE.WebGLRenderer({ canvas, antialias: true, alpha: true, powerPreference: "high-performance" }); }
catch (e) { document.body.classList.add("nogl"); throw e; }
renderer.setPixelRatio(Math.min(devicePixelRatio || 1, 1.75));
renderer.toneMapping = THREE.NeutralToneMapping;
renderer.toneMappingExposure = 0.95;
renderer.shadowMap.enabled = true;
renderer.shadowMap.type = THREE.PCFSoftShadowMap;
const reduce = matchMedia("(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)").matches;

const scene = new THREE.Scene();
const pmrem = new THREE.PMREMGenerator(renderer);
scene.environment = pmrem.fromScene(new RoomEnvironment(), 0.04).texture;
const camera = new THREE.PerspectiveCamera(32, 1, 0.05, 60);
const key = new THREE.DirectionalLight(0xffffff, 1.3);
key.position.set(3.2, 7, 4.2);
key.castShadow = true;
key.shadow.mapSize.set(2048, 2048);
Object.assign(key.shadow.camera, { left: -4.2, right: 4.2, top: 4.2, bottom: -4.2, near: 1, far: 20 });
key.shadow.bias = -0.0004; key.shadow.normalBias = 0.02; key.shadow.radius = 3;
scene.add(key, key.target);

// ---------------- the room ----------------
const FLOOR_Y = -0.75, BASE_Z = -0.3, BLOCK = 0.05;
const floorMat = new THREE.ShadowMaterial({ opacity: 0.16 });
const floor = new THREE.Mesh(new THREE.PlaneGeometry(60, 60), floorMat);
floor.rotation.x = -Math.PI / 2; floor.position.y = FLOOR_Y; floor.receiveShadow = true;
scene.add(floor);

// Cell-local layout (meters). The arm base sits at the back edge, reaching toward +z.
const START = { x: -0.15, z: 0.08, h: 0.07 };                      // taped training region (half-size)
const TARGETS = [{ x: -0.22, z: 0.3 }, { x: 0, z: 0.3 }, { x: 0.22, z: 0.3 }];
const STD_TARGET = 2;
const SPX = 1.55, SPZ = 1.35, STEP = 0.2, N = 16, HERO = 5;

// Cutting-mat texture: printed 1 cm / 5 cm grid, like the mats on real eval tables.
function matTexture() {
  const c = document.createElement("canvas"); c.width = c.height = 1024;
  const g = c.getContext("2d");
  g.fillStyle = "#2e3236"; g.fillRect(0, 0, 1024, 1024);
  for (let i = 0; i <= 100; i++) {
    const p = (i / 100) * 1024;
    g.strokeStyle = i % 5 === 0 ? "rgba(210,220,225,.20)" : "rgba(210,220,225,.07)";
    g.lineWidth = i % 5 === 0 ? 1.6 : 1;
    g.beginPath(); g.moveTo(p, 0); g.lineTo(p, 1024); g.moveTo(0, p); g.lineTo(1024, p); g.stroke();
  }
  const t = new THREE.CanvasTexture(c); t.colorSpace = THREE.SRGBColorSpace; t.anisotropy = 8; return t;
}

const M = {
  top: new THREE.MeshStandardMaterial({ color: 0xd8d2c6, roughness: 0.75 }),
  mat: new THREE.MeshStandardMaterial({ map: matTexture(), roughness: 0.9 }),
  alu: new THREE.MeshStandardMaterial({ color: 0xb4b8bc, roughness: 0.38, metalness: 0.75 }),
  tape: new THREE.MeshStandardMaterial({ color: 0x3a6cc9, roughness: 0.8 }),
  dark: new THREE.MeshStandardMaterial({ color: 0x1d2024, roughness: 0.5 }),
  lens: new THREE.MeshStandardMaterial({ color: 0x0b0c0e, roughness: 0.15, metalness: 0.3 }),
  wood: new THREE.MeshStandardMaterial({ color: 0xb98e5a, roughness: 0.72 }),
  lamp: new THREE.MeshBasicMaterial({ color: 0xffffff }),
};

// Instanced props: each entry is a geometry + material + a list of cell-local matrices.
const props = [];
const m4 = (x, y, z, sx = 1, sy = 1, sz = 1, ry = 0, rx = 0) =>
  new THREE.Matrix4().compose(new THREE.Vector3(x, y, z), new THREE.Quaternion().setFromEuler(new THREE.Euler(rx, ry, 0)), new THREE.Vector3(sx, sy, sz));
function addProp(geo, mat, locals, { shadow = true, colors = false } = {}) {
  const mesh = new THREE.InstancedMesh(geo, mat, N * locals.length);
  mesh.castShadow = shadow; mesh.receiveShadow = true; mesh.frustumCulled = false;
  if (colors) mesh.instanceColor = new THREE.InstancedBufferAttribute(new Float32Array(N * locals.length * 3).fill(0.3), 3);
  scene.add(mesh);
  const p = { mesh, locals }; props.push(p); return p;
}
const box = new THREE.BoxGeometry(1, 1, 1);
const cyl = new THREE.CylinderGeometry(1, 1, 1, 24);
addProp(box, M.top, [m4(0, -0.02, 0.0, 1.12, 0.04, 0.84)]);
addProp(box, M.mat, [m4(0, 0.001, 0.08, 0.9, 0.002, 0.56)], { shadow: false });
const legs = addProp(box, M.alu, [[-0.52, -0.38], [0.52, -0.38], [-0.52, 0.38], [0.52, 0.38]].map(([x, z]) => m4(x, -0.4, z, 0.04, 0.7, 0.04)));
legs.dynamic = true;
// Blue painter's tape L-marks: corners of the training square and the three targets.
const tapeLocals = [];
function lMarks(cx, cz, h, len = 0.035, w = 0.012) {
  for (const sx of [-1, 1]) for (const sz of [-1, 1]) {
    const x = cx + sx * h, z = cz + sz * h;
    tapeLocals.push(m4(x - sx * (len / 2 - w / 2), 0.0025, z, len, 0.001, w));
    tapeLocals.push(m4(x, 0.0025, z - sz * (len / 2 - w / 2), w, 0.001, len));
  }
}
lMarks(START.x, START.z, START.h);
TARGETS.forEach((t) => lMarks(t.x, t.z, 0.045));
addProp(box, M.tape, tapeLocals, { shadow: false });
// Masking-tape labels, written in marker, like operators do.
function tapeLabel(text) {
  const c = document.createElement("canvas"); c.width = 512; c.height = 112;
  const g = c.getContext("2d");
  g.fillStyle = "#ece6d3"; g.fillRect(0, 0, 512, 112);
  g.fillStyle = "rgba(0,0,0,.06)"; for (let i = 0; i < 512; i += 7) g.fillRect(i, 0, 2, 112);
  g.fillStyle = "#1d2024"; g.font = "700 64px 'Source Sans 3', Arial, sans-serif"; g.textAlign = "center"; g.textBaseline = "middle";
  g.save(); g.translate(256, 60); g.rotate(-0.025); g.fillText(text, 0, 0); g.restore();
  const t = new THREE.CanvasTexture(c); t.colorSpace = THREE.SRGBColorSpace; t.anisotropy = 8;
  return new THREE.MeshStandardMaterial({ map: t, roughness: 0.85 });
}
const labelGeo = new THREE.PlaneGeometry(1, 1).rotateX(-Math.PI / 2);
addProp(labelGeo, tapeLabel("TRAINING"), [m4(START.x, 0.0035, START.z + START.h + 0.032, 0.115, 1, 0.025, 0.04)], { shadow: false });
addProp(labelGeo, tapeLabel("CUE"), [m4(TARGETS[0].x - 0.075, 0.0035, TARGETS[0].z + 0.072, 0.06, 1, 0.022, -0.03)], { shadow: false });
// Wrist-height camera on a post, aimed at the workspace.
addProp(cyl, M.alu, [m4(0.5, 0.2, -0.32, 0.009, 0.4, 0.009)]);
addProp(box, M.dark, [m4(0.5, 0.415, -0.32, 0.075, 0.032, 0.03, -Math.PI * 0.22, -0.55)]);
// Tower light at the back corner: off, green after a success, red after a failure.
addProp(cyl, M.alu, [m4(-0.5, 0.09, -0.33, 0.007, 0.18, 0.007)]);
const tower = addProp(cyl, M.lamp, [m4(-0.5, 0.195, -0.33, 0.015, 0.032, 0.015)], { shadow: false, colors: true });
// Cue lights behind each target (used by the memory task).
const cues = addProp(cyl, M.lamp, TARGETS.map((t) => m4(t.x, 0.004, t.z + 0.072, 0.014, 0.006, 0.014)), { shadow: false, colors: true });
// Blocks.
const blockMesh = new THREE.InstancedMesh(new THREE.BoxGeometry(BLOCK, BLOCK, BLOCK), M.wood, N);
blockMesh.castShadow = blockMesh.receiveShadow = true; blockMesh.frustumCulled = false; scene.add(blockMesh);
// Hero-only cursor mark: a thin chalk ring on the mat.
const chalkMat = new THREE.MeshBasicMaterial({ color: 0xe9e6dc, transparent: true, opacity: 0 });
const chalk = new THREE.Mesh(new THREE.RingGeometry(0.018, 0.022, 40), chalkMat);
chalk.rotation.x = -Math.PI / 2; scene.add(chalk);

// ---------------- the robot ----------------
const gltf = await new GLTFLoader().loadAsync("/models/panda.glb");
const NAMES = ["link0", "link1", "link2", "link3", "link4", "link5", "link6", "link7", "hand", "left_finger", "right_finger"];
gltf.scene.updateMatrixWorld(true);
const bodyObj = NAMES.map((n) => gltf.scene.getObjectByName(n));
// Kinematic tree from the Menagerie MJCF (pos, quat as w x y z). The GLB's own node transforms
// include quantization offsets, so the joint math uses these values instead.
const HJ = { type: "hinge", axis: [0, 0, 1] }, SJ = { type: "slide", axis: [0, 1, 0] };
const MJCF = [
  [-1, [0, 0, 0], [1, 0, 0, 0], null], [0, [0, 0, 0.333], [1, 0, 0, 0], HJ], [1, [0, 0, 0], [1, -1, 0, 0], HJ],
  [2, [0, -0.316, 0], [1, 1, 0, 0], HJ], [3, [0.0825, 0, 0], [1, 1, 0, 0], HJ], [4, [-0.0825, 0.384, 0], [1, -1, 0, 0], HJ],
  [5, [0, 0, 0], [1, 1, 0, 0], HJ], [6, [0.088, 0, 0], [1, 1, 0, 0], HJ], [7, [0, 0, 0.107], [0.9238795, 0, 0, -0.3826834], null],
  [8, [0, 0, 0.0584], [1, 0, 0, 0], SJ], [8, [0, 0, 0.0584], [0, 0, 0, 1], SJ],
];
const bodies = MJCF.map(([parent, pos, q, joint]) => ({
  parent, pos: new THREE.Vector3(...pos), quat: new THREE.Quaternion(q[1], q[2], q[3], q[0]).normalize(), joint,
}));
let qi = 0;
for (const b of bodies) if (b.joint) { b.axis = new THREE.Vector3().fromArray(b.joint.axis).normalize(); if (b.joint.type === "hinge") b.qi = qi++; }
const bodyIndex = new Map(bodyObj.map((o, i) => [o, i]));
const matCache = new Map();
const tune = (m) => {
  if (matCache.has(m)) return matCache.get(m);
  const t = m.clone();
  if (t.color.r > 0.5) { t.color.setRGB(0.74, 0.745, 0.75); t.roughness = 0.42; }
  else if (t.color.r < 0.3) { t.color.setRGB(0.04, 0.042, 0.046); t.roughness = 0.5; }
  t.metalness = 0;
  matCache.set(m, t); return t;
};
const parts = [];
const restW = (() => { const out = bodies.map(() => new THREE.Matrix4()), m = new THREE.Matrix4();
  bodies.forEach((b, i) => { m.compose(b.pos, b.quat, new THREE.Vector3(1, 1, 1)); if (b.parent < 0) out[i].copy(m); else out[i].multiplyMatrices(out[b.parent], m); });
  return out; })();
gltf.scene.traverse((o) => {
  if (!o.isMesh) return;
  let p = o; while (p && !bodyIndex.has(p)) p = p.parent;
  if (!p) return;
  const local = restW[bodyIndex.get(p)].clone().invert().multiply(o.matrixWorld);
